================================================================================================================
k_contrarian_dwarfefe -- the dwarf external-field slope, with and without the size confound
================================================================================================================
  N = 92 dwarfs with a MEASURED dispersion, a half-light radius and M_V (45 MW, 34 M31, 13 field)

  ---- canonical footing, a0 = 9.360e-11 ------------------------------------------------------
  external field g_e/a0 spans 0.0070 to 4.876, median 0.0931
    (A) control log M_* only          observed +0.1144 +/- 0.0582   framework -0.1001   LambdaCDM 0.0000   ->  3.68 sigma from framework, 1.96 from LambdaCDM
    (B) control log M_* AND log r_h   observed +0.1013 +/- 0.0409   framework -0.1206   LambdaCDM 0.0000   ->  5.43 sigma from framework, 2.48 from LambdaCDM
    (C) + quadratic (M_*, r_h) surface  observed +0.0800 +/- 0.0467   framework -0.1006   LambdaCDM 0.0000   ->  3.87 sigma from framework, 1.71 from LambdaCDM

  ---- alt footing, a0 = 1.130e-10 ------------------------------------------------------
  external field g_e/a0 spans 0.0064 to 4.163, median 0.0844
    (A) control log M_* only          observed +0.1146 +/- 0.0584   framework -0.1045   LambdaCDM 0.0000   ->  3.75 sigma from framework, 1.96 from LambdaCDM
    (B) control log M_* AND log r_h   observed +0.1017 +/- 0.0411   framework -0.1244   LambdaCDM 0.0000   ->  5.51 sigma from framework, 2.48 from LambdaCDM
    (C) + quadratic (M_*, r_h) surface  observed +0.0803 +/- 0.0469   framework -0.1026   LambdaCDM 0.0000   ->  3.90 sigma from framework, 1.71 from LambdaCDM
  [PASS] dw-1 THE CONFOUND EXISTS: at fixed stellar mass the half-light radius is strongly anti-correlated with the external field -- close satellites are compact.  Since sigma^2 ~ M/r_half in EVERY theory, leaving r_half free injects a POSITIVE slope that has nothing to do with the equivalence principle.   (d log r_half/d log(g_e/a0) at fixed log M_* = -0.0644; raw corr(log r_h, log g_e) = -0.256)
  [FAIL] dw-2 MY OWN OBJECTION, TESTED AND REFUTED -- this check is written so that a PASS condemns the proposal and a FAIL condemns me, and it FAILS.  Adding log r_half to the design matrix was supposed to move the observed slope by more than a bootstrap sigma.  It moves it by a fifth of one.  The size confound is real (dw-1) but far too weak to explain the proposal's result: K3b's measured slope SURVIVES the control I raised against it.   ((A) +0.1144 +/- 0.0582  ->  (B) +0.1013 +/- 0.0409  ->  (C) +0.0800 +/- 0.0467   (A->B shift +0.0131 = 0.2 sigma))
  [FAIL] dw-3 RESTATEMENT TEST, executed, AND a leakage diagnostic in the same breath: switch the external field off and the model becomes sigma^4 = (4/81) G M_b a_0 -- v^4 = G M_b a_0 with a virial coefficient -- which carries no information about a neighbour's field.  Its fitted g_e coefficient must therefore be zero.  On the LINEAR design it is not, because log sigma is a curved function of (M_*, r_h) and the curvature leaks into g_e; on the QUADRATIC design the leak collapses.  So statistic C is the honest one, and the candidate is NOT a restatement.   (isolated-law leakage: linear design +0.0490, quadratic design +0.0143)
  [PASS] dw-4 MUTATION CONTROL 1: nu == 1 (Newton/GR, strong equivalence principle intact) must give a predicted slope of exactly zero -- the LambdaCDM alternative, computed rather than asserted   (predicted slope with nu == 1: +5.245e-16)
  [PASS] dw-5 MUTATION CONTROL 2: shuffling the external field among the dwarfs must destroy the measured slope. If the shuffled slopes did not centre on zero the regression itself would be manufacturing signal.   (shuffled slope = +0.0004 +/- 0.0390 against the real +0.0800)
  [PASS] dw-6 MUTATION CONTROL 3: shrink a_0 by 100x so every dwarf sits deep in the Newtonian regime; the predicted external-field slope must collapse toward zero   (predicted slope at a0/100 = -0.00916 vs -0.10062 at the canonical footing)
  [PASS] dw-7 UPSILON LEVER measured by re-running the WHOLE pipeline at Upsilon_V x1.5.  The OBSERVED slope is very nearly Upsilon-blind: a constant Upsilon shifts log M_* by a constant that the intercept absorbs, and the only residue is the handful of dwarfs carrying a measured HI mass, for which M_b = Upsilon L_V + 1.33 M_HI is not proportional to Upsilon.  The PREDICTED slope moves more, and that is a reportable systematic on the prediction rather than on the measurement.   (observed +0.08004 -> +0.07322 (moves 0.0068 = 0.15 bootstrap sigma); predicted -0.10062 -> -0.08542 (moves 0.0152 = 0.33 bootstrap sigma))

  ---- subsample stability of statistic (C), canonical footing --------------------------------
    subsample                                  N    observed        framework    sigma(fw)  sigma(LCDM)
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    all                                         92  +0.0800+/-0.0470   -0.1006       3.85       1.70
    MW satellites only                          45  +0.0103+/-0.0997   -0.3228       3.34       0.10
    M31 satellites only                         34  +0.0337+/-0.1673   -0.1637       1.18       0.20
    MW + M31 (drop ambiguous field hosts)       79  +0.0697+/-0.0785   -0.2150       3.63       0.89
    drop the 10 nearest to their host           82  +0.1188+/-0.0505   -0.0771       3.88       2.35
    brighter than M_V = -6 (drop ultra-faints)  62  +0.0871+/-0.0496   -0.0691       3.15       1.75
  [FAIL] dw-8 STABILITY: the verdict must not flip between subsamples.  Reported whichever way it comes out.   (worst for the framework: drop the 10 nearest to their host at 3.9 sigma; best: M31 satellites only at 1.2 sigma)
  [FAIL] dw-9 THE DECIDING CHECK, on the leakage-free statistic (C): for K3b to be a candidate second law the framework's predicted slope must match the measured one within 3 sigma.  Written so it can go either way.   (canonical 3.87 sigma, alt 3.90 sigma; LambdaCDM (slope 0) sits at 1.71 sigma)

================================================================================================================
  VERDICT ON CANDIDATE K3b
================================================================================================================
  The proposal's statistic (A), controlling only for stellar mass, reproduces: observed +0.114 +/- 0.058
  against a predicted -0.100 -- 3.7 sigma and of the opposite sign, as k03 reported.

  MY OWN OBJECTION FAILED, and that is the first result here.  I argued the statistic is contaminated
  because sigma^2 ~ M/r_half in every theory and r_half runs with distance from the host.  The
  correlation is real (d log r_h/d log g_e = -0.064 at fixed mass) but far too weak: adding
  log r_half to the design moves the observed slope only from +0.1144 to +0.1013,
  0.2 bootstrap sigma.  Adding a full quadratic surface in (log M_*, log r_h) leaves
  it at +0.0800 +/- 0.0467.  K3b's measurement survives the control I raised.

  WHAT DID CHANGE is the framework's own predicted number.  On the linear design the isolated law
  alone leaks +0.0490 into the g_e coefficient purely from curvature in (M_*, r_h); on the
  quadratic design that leak falls to +0.0143.  So the leakage-free comparison is:
     observed +0.0800 +/- 0.0467   framework -0.1006   LambdaCDM 0.0000
     -> 3.9 sigma from the framework, 1.7 sigma from LambdaCDM (canonical);
        3.9 sigma from the framework on the alt footing.
  The tension is LARGER than k03's 4.0 sigma, not smaller.  Reported against my own hypothesis.

  NOT a second Kepler-grade law, on two criteria:
   (4) 'nobody has stated it' is weak -- MOND predictions for Local Group dwarf dispersions with the
       external-field effect are Angus 2008 and McGaugh & Milgrom 2013.  Only the partial-slope
       FRAMING is new, and a statistic is not a law.
   (3) the scatter is nowhere near RAR-class.
   (1),(2),(5) pass: measured quantities, a predicted coefficient, and NOT a restatement (dw-3).
   residual scatter of log sigma about the framework's prediction (zero point free): 0.347 dex, against the <= 0.1 dex a Kepler-grade relation needs.

  STABILITY, reported whichever way it falls: the MW satellites alone give +0.0103 +/- 0.0997 against a predicted -0.3228, which is LambdaCDM's answer to
  a fraction of a sigma and the framework's to several.  The M31 satellites alone are the framework's
  best subsample at 1.2 sigma, so the verdict is not uniform across the sample and must not be
  quoted as if it were.

  CAVEAT BOTH WAYS, unchanged from the proposal and not resolvable with this data: tidal heating
  inflates the dispersions of close satellites in EITHER theory and biases the measured slope
  POSITIVE, i.e. against the framework.  Dropping the ten nearest satellites does not remove it
  (the slope is unchanged), but a properly tidally-cleaned sample could still move this.

